; A HASH TABLE, WRITTEN IN OROBOROS, FOR THE TARGET THAT HAS NONE.
;
; Go, JavaScript and the JVM all bring a map and we parasitize it. windows
; brings none, and CLAUDE.md's rule is explicit: a target does not get to
; decline a language construct, and if a host has no native form we build one.
; So this is the first library the language writes for itself, and it is a real
; test of general-purpose.md's claim rather than an illustration of it.
;
; It is Oroboros source and not Go for the reason target files are data: adding
; a case to `emit/*.go` for something that is a LIBRARY is the single most
; common way to get this architecture wrong. It is embedded rather than sitting
; in `lib/` only because a program must not have to import it -- `build-map` is
; the language's, like `if`.
;
; THE REPRESENTATION is one buffer, stride 3, which is tree.oro's node table
; with a different payload:
;
;   3s+0  used   0 empty, 1 occupied
;   3s+1  key
;   3s+2  value
;
; `build` ZERO-FILLS (tables.md §14.3), so "empty" needs no initialising pass --
; the same guarantee that lets tree.oro use node 0 as its `none` sentinel.
;
; A separate `used` cell rather than a reserved key value, because there is no
; integer a key cannot be: every sentinel-in-the-key-space scheme has to steal
; one, and stealing one from `int` would make `(map int V)` a lie. One word per
; slot is the honest price.
;
; OPEN ADDRESSING WITH LINEAR PROBING, and the slot count is a POWER OF TWO so
; the hash is a mask. That matters twice: `x64.and` is one instruction where a
; remainder is a division, and masking a NEGATIVE key still lands in [0, n)
; because the mask clears the sign bits. `-1 and 7` is 7.
;
; THE PROBE IS BOUNDED BY n, which is not a safety net but the algorithm: with
; the load factor held at 1/2 by `build-map`'s capacity, n probes is more than
; enough to visit every slot, and a bounded loop is one size-change termination
; can prove. An unbounded `while` could not be written here at all.
;
; WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT IS FULL is a question the program is made to answer,
; which is maps.md §6's whole argument. Insert into a full table DROPS the
; entry. That is a real semantics and not an error path we forgot: the capacity
; is declared, exceeding it is a program bug, and windows has no way to grow.
; `tree.oro`'s node cap is the same shape and CLAUDE.md celebrates it -- a
; capacity does not create the limit, it makes it visible.

(module win/map)
(use x64)

; The slot count, read back off the buffer rather than threaded through every
; call. `(len m)` is 3n by construction, so there is nothing to carry.
(def wm-n (fn (m) (x64.idiv (len m) 3)))

; The slot for key k at probe distance i, masked into [0, n).
(def wm-s (fn (n k i) (x64.and (x64.add (x64.and k (x64.sub n 1)) i) (x64.sub n 1))))

; A CLAMP, exactly as tree.oro uses one. `wm-s` is masked and therefore already
; in range, but the refinement layer reasons in linear integer arithmetic and
; `x64.and` is not linear -- so the fact is true and undischargeable, which is
; the honest limit of the domain rather than a hole in the program. Clamping
; turns a refusal into a note and says what happens if the impossible occurs.
(def wm-c (fn (m x) (if (x64.setl x 0) 0 (if (x64.setge x (len m)) 0 x))))

(def wm-used (fn (m n k i) (m (wm-c m (x64.imul 3 (wm-s n k i))))))
(def wm-key  (fn (m n k i) (m (wm-c m (x64.add (x64.imul 3 (wm-s n k i)) 1)))))

; The index of the VALUE cell, which is what a find returns so the caller needs
; no second computation.
(def wm-vi (fn (m n k i) (wm-c m (x64.add (x64.imul 3 (wm-s n k i)) 2))))

; FIND: the value cell's index, or -1 when the key is absent.
;
; Three exits and no `again` under an `if`, which is ADR 0015 choosing the shape
; and choosing better: the guards ARE the control flow, so the probe reads as
; the three things that can happen at a slot rather than as nested conditionals.
;
;   ran out of probes  -> absent
;   an EMPTY slot      -> absent, because linear probing never leaves a gap
;                         before a key it stored
;   the key            -> found
(def wm-find (fn (m k)
  (let (wm-n m) (fn (n)
    (loop ((i 0))
      (x64.setge i n)              -1
      (= (wm-used m n k i) 0)      -1
      (= (wm-key m n k i) k)       (wm-vi m n k i)
      else                         (again (x64.add i 1)))))))

; INSERT. The same three-way probe, with the buffer threaded through so ADR
; 0018's linearity holds: every clause body either returns the buffer or hands
; it to `again`.
; EVERY INDEX IS COMPUTED BEFORE THE FIRST STORE, and that is ADR 0018's
; linearity showing up as a shape requirement rather than as a rule to recite.
;
; `(set b i v)` CONSUMES b, so `(set (set m A 1) (wm-c m ...) k)` is a use of m
; after it was handed on -- the clamp needs `(len m)` and the buffer is already
; gone. The compiler refused the first version of this function for exactly
; that, which is the linearity check doing its job on the first library the
; language wrote for itself.
;
; Binding the indices first is not a workaround: it is the honest order, since
; every index depends only on n, k and i, and none of them on the stores.
; `n` IS READ OFF THE LOOP'S OWN BUFFER, not bound outside it, and that is
; linearity again rather than a style choice. `(let (wm-n m) (fn (n) (loop ((m m)
; …))))` uses the incoming buffer TWICE at the top -- once to measure it and
; once to hand it to the loop -- and when the argument is itself a `wm-put`,
; call-by-need binds it and the second use is a use after hand-off. Reading it
; inside, where the loop variable is the buffer, is one use.
;
; It costs a division per probe step, which the host hoists out of a loop whose
; buffer does not change length; if that ever measures, `n` becomes a parameter.
(def wm-put (fn (m k v)
  (loop ((m m) (i 0))
      ; Full. The entry is DROPPED -- see the header.
      (x64.setge i (wm-n m))          m
      ; A free slot: claim it.
      (= (wm-used m (wm-n m) k i) 0)
        (let (wm-c m (x64.imul 3 (wm-s (wm-n m) k i))) (fn (u)
          (let (wm-c m (x64.add (x64.imul 3 (wm-s (wm-n m) k i)) 1)) (fn (kc)
            (let (wm-vi m (wm-n m) k i) (fn (vc)
              (set (set (set m u 1) kc k) vc v)))))))
      ; The key is already here: overwrite, so an insert is idempotent in the
      ; domain. This is the clause that makes |dom m| an INTERVAL rather than an
      ; equation (growth.md §1.1) -- whether the key was present is a fact about
      ; the input, and here is where the program finds out.
      (= (wm-key m (wm-n m) k i) k)
        (let (wm-vi m (wm-n m) k i) (fn (vc) (set m vc v)))
      else                     (again m (x64.add i 1)))))

; HOW MANY KEYS ARE PRESENT. A scan, because nothing counts as it goes.
;
; O(n) where Go and Java are O(1), which is the same per-target cost JavaScript
; pays with `Object.keys(m).length` -- the ANSWER is identical, so it is not a
; Tier 2 disagreement, only a different price. A count in a reserved header cell
; would make it O(1) and is not done here because no program needs it yet.
(def wm-len (fn (m)
  (let (wm-n m) (fn (n)
    (loop ((c 0) (s 0))
      (x64.setge s n)  c
      else             (again (x64.add c (m (wm-c m (x64.imul 3 s)))) (x64.add s 1)))))))

; THE SLOT COUNT FROM A CAPACITY: the least power of two that is at least twice
; the capacity, so the load factor never exceeds 1/2 and linear probing stays
; short. Computed with a loop because the capacity may be a runtime value; when
; it is a literal, reduction folds the whole thing away.
(def wm-slots (fn (cap)
  (loop ((n 1))
    (x64.setge n (x64.imul 2 cap))  n
    else                            (again (x64.imul n 2)))))

; FILL a table from two parallel arrays, which is how a map LITERAL is built.
;
; One `wm-put` call site rather than one per row, and that is a host constraint
; rather than taste: each inlined `wm-put` is a probe loop holding a buffer, an
; index and a slot, and nesting them past two exhausts x86's scratch registers
; -- the same wall `len-bounded.oro` caps itself at three inputs to avoid. A
; loop has constant register pressure however many rows there are.
(def wm-fill (fn (m ks vs)
  (loop ((m m) (i 0))
    (x64.setge i (len ks))  m
    ; THE VALUE INDEX IS CLAMPED, because the loop is bounded by `(len ks)` and
    ; indexes `vs` as well -- two arrays under one bound, which is exactly the
    ; latent bug the refinement layer found in `dot` and `centroid`. The two
    ; arrays have the same length by construction here and nothing can see that,
    ; so the program says what happens if they do not.
    else                    (again (wm-put m (ks i) (vs (wm-c vs i))) (x64.add i 1)))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- keys
;
; `keys m` is the map's keys in ASCENDING order, and the order is what the
; construct is FOR: a host's iteration order is not canonical -- Go randomises
; on purpose, JavaScript specifies its own, Java leaves it unspecified -- so
; sorting by the order on K is what makes the answer the same on four hosts.
; Here there is no host order at all, only slot order, which is the hash's.
;
; SORTED BY RANK, NOT BY EXCHANGE, and the reason is the map's own invariant:
; A MAP'S KEYS ARE DISTINCT, because the index set is a SET. So the sorted
; position of a key is exactly the number of keys below it, and every key can be
; written straight to its place in one pass. No comparisons between output
; slots, no swap, no shifting.
;
; That is O(n^2) in comparisons and O(n) in stores, which is honest rather than
; good -- the price of an order on the host that has none. The other three call
; their own sort, which is the rule about emitting at the highest layer the
; target provides. A counting sort over int keys is the obvious improvement and
; is not made until a program measures it.
;
; It also avoids a SWAP, and that is not a stylistic choice: a swap reads two
; slots and then writes both, and the compiler currently substitutes a buffer
; read past a store, so `b[x], b[y] = b[y], b[x]` emits as `b[x] = b[y];
; b[y] = b[x]` and silently copies. That is a real bug in the effect discipline
; -- ADR 0010 exists to forbid exactly that reordering, and a buffer read is not
; being judged impure. Ranking needs no swap and is correct either way.

(def wm-used-at (fn (m s) (m (wm-c m (x64.imul 3 s)))))
(def wm-key-at  (fn (m s) (m (wm-c m (x64.add (x64.imul 3 s) 1)))))

; How many occupied keys are strictly less than k. With distinct keys that IS
; the key's index in ascending order.
(def wm-rank (fn (m k)
  (loop ((c 0) (s 0))
    (x64.setge s (wm-n m))     c
    (= (wm-used-at m s) 0)     (again c (x64.add s 1))
    else                       (again (if (x64.setl (wm-key-at m s) k) (x64.add c 1) c)
                                      (x64.add s 1)))))

; One pass over the slots, each occupied key written to its own rank. `out` is
; only ever WRITTEN here and `m` only ever READ, so no store can shadow a read.
(def wm-keys (fn (m)
  (let (wm-len m) (fn (cnt)
    (build cnt (fn (out)
      (loop ((out out) (s 0))
        (x64.setge s (wm-n m))   out
        (= (wm-used-at m s) 0)   (again out (x64.add s 1))
        else                     (again (set out (wm-c out (wm-rank m (wm-key-at m s)))
                                              (wm-key-at m s))
                                        (x64.add s 1)))))))))
